"# Help and flag placement\n\nA command has levels: the program, its subcommand, the action (`aos-agent`, `ctl`, `stop`). A flag belongs to one level and may go anywhere after that level's name, also after later names and arguments. These are the same:\n\n```sh\naos-agent -d /srv/d ctl stop bob\naos-agent ctl stop bob -d /srv/d\naos-agent ctl stop -t bob\naos-tools add --name now tools/clock\naos-tools add tools/clock --name now\n```\n\n`aos-agent ctl` start / stop / sleep / wake / pause / resume / where take the agent as `-t NAME` or as the last argument. say, listen, tools, llm, history and hook take only `-t`. `ctl add / rm / ls / who` take no `-t`.\n\nText that is handed on as it is ends flag reading: everything from the first word of `aos-agent say WORDS`, `history summarize RANGE TEXT`, `history set LINE JSON`, `aos-mailbox send WHAT`, `aos-clock-register add NAME TEXT` is text, so put flags before it. After `--` nothing is a flag: `aos-daemon-ctl add tools/job -- --verbose`, `aos-agent say -t bob -- -hello`.\n\nThis holds for aos-agent, aos-daemon-ctl, aos-grow, aos-tools, aos-mailbox and aos-clock-register. Other commands (aos-mcp, aos-sandbox, aos-clock, aos-sweeper, aos-eca-screen...) still stop reading flags at the first positional argument: there put flags before it.\n\nRun `aos-agent --help`, then `aos-agent tools --help`, then `aos-agent tools add --help`. Each prints only its own level. Help needs no live daemon or target.\n\nCommon default short names are: `-h --help`, `-d --daemon`, `-t --target`, `-j --json`, `-n --name`, `-w --ws`, `-s --stack`, `-f --follow`, `-y --yes`. Each command defines its own short names and may give them different meanings. Only use a short name where that help lists it. `--file`, `--detach` and `--timeout-s` have no short name. `-s`/`-w` are not aliases for the switches in `ctl where`, or the path in `ctl who`.\n\nUse separate tokens: `-t bob`. `-tbob` and `-jf` are unknown flags. A flag nobody in the command knows gives `FlagUnknown`. No subcommand gives `ArgumentsBad`; an unknown subcommand gives `CommandUnknown`.\n"
